window.docContents[2].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Examples-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Example2___9___8":{"contents":"Example 2.9.8. Some examples of representations of Lie algebras are:\n\n(1) V = 0.\n\n(2) Any vector space V with \\rho = 0 (the trivial representation).\n\n(3) The adjoint representation V = \\mathfrak{g} with \\rho(a)(b) := [a, b]. That this is a representation follows from equation (2.9.1). Thus, the meaning of the Jacobi identity is that it is equivalent to the existence of the adjoint representation.\n\nIt turns out that a representation of a Lie algebra \\mathfrak{g} is the same thing as a representation of a certain associative algebra U(\\mathfrak{g}). Thus, as with quivers, we can view the theory of representations of Lie algebras as a part of the theory of representations of associative algebras.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.9. Lie algebras\tExamples of representations of Lie algebras","header":"Examples of representations of Lie algebras","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Examples-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Example2___9___8"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Kostka-numbers-K____mu___lambda___/#Chapter5___Definition5___14___2":{"contents":"Definition 5.14.2. The integers K_{\\mu\\lambda} are called the Kostka numbers.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.14. Induced representations for $S_n$\tKostka numbers K_{mu,lambda}","header":"Kostka numbers K_{mu,lambda}","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Kostka-numbers-K____mu___lambda___/#Chapter5___Definition5___14___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___6___-Representations-of-products/Section-5___6___-Representations-of-products/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___6":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.6. Representations of products\tSection 5.6: Representations of products","header":"Section 5.6: Representations of products","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___6___-Representations-of-products/Section-5___6___-Representations-of-products/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___6"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___4___-Reflection-functor-F_i___-/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___4":{"contents":"Definition 6.6.4. Let Q be a quiver, and let i \\in Q be a source. Let V be a representation of Q. Let \\psi be the canonical map\n\n\\psi : V_i \\to \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j.\n\nThen we define the reflection functor\n\nF_i^- : \\operatorname{Rep} Q \\to \\operatorname{Rep} \\overline{Q}_i\n\nby the rule\n\nF_i^-(V)_k = V_k \\quad \\text{if } k \\neq i,\n\nF_i^-(V)_i = \\operatorname{Coker}(\\psi) = \\left( \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j \\right) / \\operatorname{Im} \\psi.\n\nAgain, all maps stay the same except those now pointing into i; these are replaced by the compositions of the inclusions V_k \\to \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j with the natural map \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j \\to \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j / \\operatorname{Im} \\psi.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.6. Reflection functors\tDefinition 6.6.4: Reflection functor F_i^-","header":"Definition 6.6.4: Reflection functor F_i^-","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___4___-Reflection-functor-F_i___-/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___4"}});
